   "text": {
    "L1": "An estimate is a quick, sensible answer. It is useful even when it is not exact.",
    "L2": "Here is a myth: an estimate is useless unless exact. Not true—an estimate helps us decide and check quickly.",
    "L3": "Round to friendly numbers, such as the nearest ten, hundred, or thousand. Round to the nearest number, not always down.",
    "L4": "For 18 tawas making about 42 rotis each, round 18 to 20 and 42 to 40. Then 20 times 40 gives about 800.",
    "L5": "The exact answer is 756, which is close to 800. Rounding can give a fair estimate before exact working.",
    "L6": "For mental maths, split numbers into easier parts. Think of 25 times 16 as 25 times 4 times 4, which gives 400.",
    "L7": "You can also compensate. For 99 plus 47, make 99 into 100, then take away 1: the answer is 146.",
    "L8": "Finally, compare an exact answer with your estimate. If they are far apart, check the calculation."
   },
